co, with his blend of simple faith and gipsy blood, had violated a temple, or looted a shrine, to save his son's life, and that the guardians of the relic tracked him and revenged the outrage.", "reference": "1923, Ernest Bramah, The Eyes of Max Carrados:"}, {"text": "Vlad says there is an American drone the Russians can't track. He wants a lot more of those.", "reference": "2022 May 5, Erin Burnett, 3:37 from the start, in See how gamers are outwitting and helping to kill Russian soldiers, CNN, archived from the original on 13 May 2022:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To match the movement or change of a person or object.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["My height tracks my father's at my age, so I might end up as tall as him."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive or intransitive, of a camera) To travel so that a moving object remains in shot.", "tags": ["intransitive", "transitive"], "examples": ["The camera tracked the ball even as the field of play moved back and forth, keeping the action in shot the entire time."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive, chiefly of a storm) To move.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": ["The hurricane tracked further west than expected."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To traverse; to move across.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I've swept o'er the mountain, the forest and fell, / I've played on the rock where the wild chamois dwell; / I have tracked the desert so dreary and rude, / Through the pathless depths of its solitude; […]", "reference": "1837, Elizabeth Parker, Popular Poems. Selected by E. P., page 228:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {